§§ `Regulation 87 -- Offences of prejudicing investigations`

-- Reg 87(10): the definitions of "relevant officer" and "appropriate officer".  The rendering prints the list items without letters and the line "acting pursuant to arrangements ..." after the second limb of "relevant officer" (lines 2690-2693): fork F-ARRANGEMENTS reads it as qualifying both limbs, as in regulation 65(2).
-- src:2684 | (10) For the purposes of this regulation—
-- src:2685 | “appropriate officer” means—
-- src:2686 | an officer of the FCA, including a member of the FCA's staff or an agent of the FCA;
-- src:2687 | an officer of Revenue and Customs;
-- src:2688 | an employee or agent of a professional body listed in Schedule 1 who is authorised by the body to act on behalf of the body for the purposes of this Part; or
-- src:2689 | a relevant officer;
-- src:2690 | “relevant officer” means—
-- src:2691 | in Great Britain, an officer of a local weights and measures authority;
-- src:2692 | in Northern Ireland, an officer of the Department for the Economy;
-- src:2693 | acting pursuant to arrangements made with the FCA or with the Commissioners for the purposes of these Regulations.
@ref reg 87(10) (src:2684-2693)
GIVEN `the person is an officer of a local weights and measures authority in Great Britain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an officer of the Department for the Economy in Northern Ireland` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is acting pursuant to arrangements made with the FCA or with the Commissioners for the purposes of these Regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 87(10) the person is a relevant officer` IS
    IF (`the person is an officer of a local weights and measures authority in Great Britain` OR `the person is an officer of the Department for the Economy in Northern Ireland`) AND `the person is acting pursuant to arrangements made with the FCA or with the Commissioners for the purposes of these Regulations`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“relevant officer” means— in Great Britain, an officer of a local weights and measures authority; in Northern Ireland, an officer of the Department for the Economy; acting pursuant to arrangements made with the FCA or with the Commissioners"

GIVEN `the person is an officer of the FCA, including a member of the FCA's staff or an agent of the FCA` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an officer of Revenue and Customs` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is an employee or agent of a professional body listed in Schedule 1` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is authorised by the body to act on behalf of the body for the purposes of this Part` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reg 87(10) the person is a relevant officer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 87(10) the person is an appropriate officer` IS
    IF `the person is an officer of the FCA, including a member of the FCA's staff or an agent of the FCA` OR `the person is an officer of Revenue and Customs` OR (`the person is an employee or agent of a professional body listed in Schedule 1` AND `the person is authorised by the body to act on behalf of the body for the purposes of this Part`) OR (`reg 87(10) the person is a relevant officer` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“appropriate officer” means— an officer of the FCA ...; an officer of Revenue and Customs; an employee or agent of a professional body listed in Schedule 1 who is authorised by the body ...; or a relevant officer"

-- Reg 87(1): the regulation applies if P knows or suspects that an appropriate officer is acting, or proposing to act, in connection with an investigation into a potential contravention of a relevant requirement which is being, or is about to be, conducted.
-- src:2657 | (1) This regulation applies if a person (“P”) knows or suspects that an appropriate officer is acting (or proposing to act) in connection with an investigation into a potential contravention of a relevant requirement which is being or is about to be conducted.
@ref reg 87(1) (src:2657)
GIVEN `P knows that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `P suspects that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reg 87(10) the person is an appropriate officer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the investigation is into a potential contravention of a relevant requirement and is being or is about to be conducted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 87(1) this regulation applies` IS
    IF (`P knows that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation` OR `P suspects that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation`) AND (`reg 87(10) the person is an appropriate officer` EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND `the investigation is into a potential contravention of a relevant requirement and is being or is about to be conducted`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF (`P knows that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation` OR `P suspects that an appropriate officer is acting or proposing to act in connection with an investigation`) AND (NOT (`reg 87(10) the person is an appropriate officer` EQUALS JUST FALSE)) AND `the investigation is into a potential contravention of a relevant requirement and is being or is about to be conducted`
         THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): an answer that another provision gives is not in
         ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This regulation applies if a person (“P”) knows or suspects that an appropriate officer is acting (or proposing to act) in connection with an investigation" [marginal (a)]

-- Reg 87(2): the two offences.
-- src:2658 | (2) P commits an offence if—
-- src:2659 | (a) P makes a disclosure which is likely to prejudice the investigation; or
-- src:2660 | (b) P falsifies, conceals, destroys or otherwise disposes of, or causes or permits the falsification, concealment, destruction or disposal of, documents which are relevant to the investigation.
@ref reg 87(2) (src:2658-2660)
GIVEN `reg 87(1) this regulation applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `P makes a disclosure which is likely to prejudice the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 87(2)(a) P commits an offence by a disclosure likely to prejudice the investigation` IS
    IF (`reg 87(1) this regulation applies` EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND `P makes a disclosure which is likely to prejudice the investigation`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF (NOT (`reg 87(1) this regulation applies` EQUALS JUST FALSE)) AND `P makes a disclosure which is likely to prejudice the investigation`
         THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): an answer that another provision gives is not in
         ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "P commits an offence if— (a) P makes a disclosure which is likely to prejudice the investigation" [marginal (a)]

GIVEN `reg 87(1) this regulation applies` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `P falsifies, conceals, destroys or otherwise disposes of, or causes or permits the falsification, concealment, destruction or disposal of, documents which are relevant to the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
DECIDE `reg 87(2)(b) P commits an offence by falsifying, concealing or destroying documents` IS
    IF (`reg 87(1) this regulation applies` EQUALS JUST TRUE) AND `P falsifies, conceals, destroys or otherwise disposes of, or causes or permits the falsification, concealment, destruction or disposal of, documents which are relevant to the investigation`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE IF (NOT (`reg 87(1) this regulation applies` EQUALS JUST FALSE)) AND `P falsifies, conceals, destroys or otherwise disposes of, or causes or permits the falsification, concealment, destruction or disposal of, documents which are relevant to the investigation`
         THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): an answer that another provision gives is not in
         ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "P commits an offence if— (b) P falsifies, conceals, destroys or otherwise disposes of" [marginal (a)]

-- Reg 87(4) and (5): "criminal conduct" and "benefits from conduct" (used by the statutes named in (3)(c) to (e)).
